00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.361
model and client that you want to use.

20
00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:05.165
And now you can launch and connect
to an instance of Phoenix.

21
00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:08.168
So Phoenix is an application
that can receive the traces

22
00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:10.236
that you're going to send
from your agent here.

23
00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.906
And then can visualize those in a UI.

24
00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:16.443
And so in this environment you've already
got an instance of Phoenix running.

25
00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:19.112
There are a few ways that you can
launch Phoenix.

26
00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.815
You can either use

27
00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.818
this command
to launch Phoenix inside of a notebook.

28
00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:27.754
Alternatively,
you can run it on your own local machine,

29
00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:30.723
or you can access it through a web
app on the Arize website.

30
00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:34.194
In this case, in these notebooks,
Phoenix has been launched for you,

31
00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:35.762
so you don't actually have to run anything
here.

32
00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:37.964
You've already got an instance
that's running.

33
00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:40.600
Now, Phoenix has a concept of projects,

34
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.904
and so projects are used to separate out
some of the traces that you're sending.

35
00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:47.974
Maybe you're tracing multiple
different kinds of applications or agents.

36
00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:51.277
You might want to group some of the
tracing into different projects.

37
00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:54.114
So here
you want to define the project name.

38
00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:57.117
And then you can use the register
method from Phoenix

39
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.054
to connect your application
here to your Phoenix instance.

40
00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:06.059
So this register method here will take in
the project name as well as an endpoint.

41
00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.496
This endpoint is just
where your Phoenix instance is running.

42
00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.831
And that will be
where any traces get sent to.

43
00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.533
So again
you have it running in your notebook.

44
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:16.369
And so this helper method here
get Phoenix endpoint.

45
00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.437
It's in the utils function.

46
00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:20.073
You can take a look
and see what that's doing if you want to.

47
00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:21.341
And that will give you an output

48
00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:23.409
that looks something like

49
00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:26.446
this where you can see OpenTelemetry
tracing has been set up.

50
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.884
And so now any openTelemetry traces
that you capture or you instrument

51
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.320
will be sent through
into your Phoenix project.

52
00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:39.492
So, next step that you'll go through here
is you've made this connection to Phoenix,

53
00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:43.363
but you still need to instrument
your application and mark which calls

54
00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:46.065
and which methods should be sent
through into Phoenix.

55
00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.834
And with what attributes.

56
00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.269
You can do this manually.

57
00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:50.436
However, libraries

58
00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:53.606
like Phoenix also have tools to do
some of this automatically for you.

59
00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:57.177
So this OpenAI instrument or for example,

60
00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:00.180
is part of the open inference library
that we mentioned.

61
00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:03.149
And what this will do is if I run that,
it will take

62
00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:06.152
in the tracer provider
that we set up here,

63
00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:10.557
and it will actually make it
so that any calls you make to OpenAI's

64
00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:14.827
library from this point on in the project
will get sent through into Phoenix

65
00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.196
traced properly.

66
00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.765
So if you're just using OpenAI calls
this would be

67
00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:21.834
all you would need to do to actually set
up tracing for those calls.

68
00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.905
In this example agent,
you have some stuff beyond that as well too.

69
00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.041
So you have tool calls that get made.

70
00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.876
You have some other logic
that's happening.

71
00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.745
So you're actually going to combine
this automatic instrumentor

72
00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:35.748
here with some more manual tracing.

73
00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:39.619
And so, to set up your manual tracing,
the first thing you want to do here

74
00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.889
is you want to get

75
00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.892
a tracer object from that tracer provider.

76
00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.895
And then this is what's going to be used
to mark any of those methods

77
00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:51.864
that you want to send through into Phoenix
as well.

78
00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.033
Now with all of that

79
00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:58.071
set up, make sure that you've also run
the different cells of your agent

80
00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:01.541
so that you can then run and test
your agent as we trace it.

81
00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:04.744
Now when it comes to

82
00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:08.581
setting up manual tracing for your agent
or really any project, it's

83
00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:11.918
always helpful to start from the outermost
layer that you want to trace.

84
00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:15.121
The outermost tracer span,
and then work your way down

85
00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.123
in terms of the detail
that you want to go to.

86
00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:19.892
This just helps make sure that you're
capturing the right information,

87
00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.762
and you give yourself a holistic view
of what's going on.

88
00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.698
So in this case,
you have your run agent method

89
00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.701
that's going to be used here
to start your agent.

90
00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:30.603
However, this method has a loop in it.

91
00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.872
And as we mentioned
in the previous notebook,

92
00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:36.075
sometimes you might want to use more of a
recursive call here where you're calling

93
00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:39.078
this method multiple times
for one given run of your agent.

94
00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:42.248
So it's helpful
sometimes to create an actual

95
00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:45.251
another outer layer
that you can use, or outer method

96
00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:48.488
that you can start
as that very top level trace or span here.

97
00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:51.057
So in this case
you're going to create a new method.

98
00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.059
In this case the start

99
00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:56.496
main span could just be called start agent
or whatever you want to use there.

100
00:04:57.163 --> 00:05:00.133
And really all this method is doing aside
from some of the tracing stuff,

101
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.269
is just calling this run
agent method that you have already.

102
00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:06.039
So it's just calling that run
agent method.

103
00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:07.840
And again,
if you had that as a recursive method,

104
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.742
this would be the initial call
that you would make to that method.

105
00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:14.247
And then beyond that it's adding
in some calls to set up tracing here.

106
00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:18.318
So this is your first manual tracing call
that's being added.

107
00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:20.887
So you'll add in using this tracer object.

108
00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:24.324
You'll start the span here with the name
Agent Run.

109
00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.759
You can make the name whenever you want.

110
00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:29.729
And then the open inference span
kind, agent.

111
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.366
The span kind is just
what kind of category is the span.

112
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.501
It'll map to some of the colors and things

113
00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.403
like that in the Phoenix
UI that you'll see in a second.

114
00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:39.605
So in this case, agent is the example.

115
00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:42.241
You'll see some for
tools and for chain later on.

116
00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:45.678
And then
that will start that current span.

117
00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:50.016
And anything within that with clause block
will be treated as part of that span.

118
00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:52.885
You can also set some attributes
for the span.

119
00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.988
So in this case setting the input to
what's the input value

120
00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:56.856
for that given span.

121
00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:59.859
In this case it'll be the messages object.

122
00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:03.463
And then when the agent completes
you can set the output here.

123
00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:06.499
This will be the value
of the return of the agent.

124
00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:10.002
And then you can set a status code
if you want to to say this was a completed

125
00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.739
call correctly.
There was not an error on this call.

126
00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:14.707
So you can define that method.

127
00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.008
And then from there

128
00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:19.212
you can start to work your way down
into the agent adding tracer.

129
00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:22.382
So next step can be done here

130
00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:25.251
is going into your run agent method.

131
00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:28.254
And there's maybe a few places
that you might want to start spans here.

132
00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:31.557
The most obvious one
is that each time you call your router,

133
00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:34.727
you may want to have a span
representing that router call.

134
00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.029
So here's what you can add to do that.

135
00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:41.667
So there's been a couple updates
to the method here.

136
00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.202
Everything up here is the same.

137
00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:45.938
No changes
there as you get down to your while loop.

138
00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:48.141
Here's the first change that you'll see.

139
00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:51.878
So adding that same call from before
tracer start this current span.

140
00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:56.983
This time calling it router call and then
adding in the open inferred span kind.

141
00:06:56.983 --> 00:07:01.921
This time adding in chain as the span kind.
Chain is just a basic logic step.

142
00:07:01.921 --> 00:07:06.259
There's no LLM call or tool call or it's
not an agent, it's just a chain. Chain is

143
00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.528
almost the default in a way.

144
00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:11.531
So you can start that
and then add in the input.

145
00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:14.434
Once again you'll keep your OpenAI

146
00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:16.068
call the same here.

147
00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:17.603
Add in your status as well.

148
00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:19.405
And then you

149
00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:22.375
can add in the output either
the tool calls

150
00:07:22.408 --> 00:07:23.709
or if you have

151
00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:27.079
all the tool calls completed and you're
going back to the user, you can add the

152
00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:30.349
eventual response of your agent here
to final response.

153
00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:33.853
So you can update that method here.
And then you can add

154
00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:36.889
a couple more pieces and then take a look
at what this looks like in Phoenix.

155
00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:40.460
So one other area
that you might want to add another

156
00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.463
span is when you go to handle the tool
calls,

157
00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:46.532
you might want to have a span
that captures that particular

158
00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:48.534
method and tracks that method.

159
00:07:48.534 --> 00:07:50.570
So here's how you can do that.

160
00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.705
You can go up to your handle tool
call method.

161
00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:56.809
And in this case you can use a different
technique for actually marking a span.

162
00:07:56.843 --> 00:07:59.512
So instead of using the with clause
you have another option

163
00:07:59.512 --> 00:08:02.014
which is to use a decorator
for this method.

164
00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:05.017
And this is really useful here
because really this method is

165
00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.118
totally self-contained.

166
00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:09.755
If you just said everything in this
method can be one span and that would work

167
00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:12.492
well for the instrumentation
you want to set up.

168
00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:13.960
So here you can just mark

169
00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:17.530
@tracer.chain.

170
00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.433
You could do @tracer agent
or @tracer tool

171
00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:23.769
for the different kinds of tool spans
or spans that you would want to track.

172
00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.840
And then what that will do is
it will treat any calls to this

173
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.344
method
right here as a single span in Phoenix.

174
00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:35.214
And then it will take the inputs to this
method as the inputs,

175
00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.985
and then it will take any return from this
method as the output value.

176
00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:43.556
So if you saw before you were marking
the input in the output manually,

177
00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:46.626
this is a kind of convenience way
that makes it

178
00:08:46.626 --> 00:08:48.327
so that you don't have to define
those manually.

179
00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.997
It'll just happen automatically.

180
00:08:50.997 --> 00:08:52.031
And so now's a good moment.

181
00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:55.268
You've added a few different kinds
of instrumentation spans and traces.

182
00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:59.205
It's a good opportunity to take a pause,
run your agent and see what that looks

183
00:08:59.205 --> 00:08:59.872
like in Phoenix.

