232
00:11:20.579 --> 00:11:21.747
And it would be helpful to understand

233
00:11:21.747 --> 00:11:24.984
when those tools are actually being called
and what the responses are there.

234
00:11:25.551 --> 00:11:28.621
So you can add in some tracing
for those tools as well.

235
00:11:29.989 --> 00:11:33.325
So to add tracing to some of your tools,
you have a few different options here.

236
00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.797
First, you can use those decorators
that you used for your handle tool

237
00:11:37.797 --> 00:11:39.699
calls method.
And that's probably the simplest way.

238
00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:42.668
So usually the recommended way
to go about doing that.

239
00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:46.138
So starting here
you have your lookup sales data method

240
00:11:46.238 --> 00:11:48.174
which is your first tool.

241
00:11:48.174 --> 00:11:50.943
And so you can add in a decorator
at the top

242
00:11:50.943 --> 00:11:53.946
to say tracer dot tool.

243
00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:54.914
And then again

244
00:11:54.914 --> 00:11:57.917
that will treat this as a single span
whenever this method is called.

245
00:11:57.917 --> 00:12:01.687
And then it will take the input
as this prompt value and the output

246
00:12:01.687 --> 00:12:03.556
as whatever the return of this method is.

247
00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:05.357
Now this

248
00:12:05.357 --> 00:12:08.728
particular tool actually calls
some other kinds of methods inside of it.

249
00:12:08.728 --> 00:12:11.430
It calls this generate SQL query method.

250
00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.767
And so it might be helpful to have
that as a separate span to say okay, first

251
00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:17.236
you want to have a span
for generating the SQL code

252
00:12:17.236 --> 00:12:20.072
and then a separate
one for running the SQL code.

253
00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:22.808
So what you can do is
if there's part of a method

254
00:12:22.808 --> 00:12:26.812
that you want to actually track
as an individual span,

255
00:12:26.812 --> 00:12:29.815
you can use the with clause
that you saw before.

256
00:12:30.116 --> 00:12:34.887
So you can add in here
before your SQL query is run.

257
00:12:34.954 --> 00:12:39.225
You can add in a with clause to say
start_as_current_span.

258
00:12:39.658 --> 00:12:42.661
In this case
you can call it execute SQL query

259
00:12:42.995 --> 00:12:46.165
and set the open inference
span kind as chain once again.

260
00:12:47.366 --> 00:12:49.702
And then you'll want to make sure
that this is indented

261
00:12:49.702 --> 00:12:52.705
so that it's part of that span
that you've created there.

262
00:12:53.339 --> 00:12:56.442
And then it's would be good to add
in some of the input and output values

263
00:12:56.442 --> 00:12:57.743
that go along with that as well.

264
00:12:57.743 --> 00:13:02.615
So, right before this
you can add in the input value.

265
00:13:02.748 --> 00:13:06.252
And so you can do span dot set input.

266
00:13:06.886 --> 00:13:09.255
And in this case the SQL query

267
00:13:09.255 --> 00:13:12.224
that's going to be called
would make sense as a good input.

268
00:13:12.224 --> 00:13:14.827
And then after your call is made you
You can say span

269
00:13:14.827 --> 00:13:17.062
set output and set status.

270
00:13:17.062 --> 00:13:20.232
With the result of that SQL query.

271
00:13:21.333 --> 00:13:24.336
And in this case you're defining it
using the parameter name.

272
00:13:24.537 --> 00:13:27.540
You can also just define it
by sending the parameter itself

273
00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:30.142
whichever way is easier for you to use.

274
00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:32.545
And then so now if you run this

275
00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:36.015
then that tool will have two spans
that it creates whenever it gets run.

276
00:13:36.048 --> 00:13:39.819
And we'll have one for the method itself
using this decorator here.

277
00:13:40.319 --> 00:13:43.889
And then it will have a separate span
within that other span nested below

278
00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:47.560
for specifically
the SQL query running itself.

279
00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:51.630
And it makes sense to do that in this case
because you might generate correct SQL,

280
00:13:51.831 --> 00:13:53.132
but then there might be some connection

281
00:13:53.132 --> 00:13:56.135
issue or something else
that prevents it from being run correctly.

282
00:13:56.235 --> 00:13:58.337
And it's helpful
to see where that error might be.

283
00:13:58.337 --> 00:14:02.508
So if you see that the internal span
here fails but the external one succeeds,

284
00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:06.145
then you know that there was an issue
with connecting to your SQL database

285
00:14:06.145 --> 00:14:07.413
in this case.

286
00:14:07.413 --> 00:14:11.150
And so finally you can add in similar
tracing to your remaining tools.

287
00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:14.854
And then that'll probably cover all the
tracing that you need for the agent here.

288
00:14:15.054 --> 00:14:18.123
And so this is fairly straightforward
to add in here.

289
00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:21.927
Your next method here
is pretty simple in this tool.

290
00:14:21.994 --> 00:14:25.331
So you can just use the decorator
to say trace this as a tool.

291
00:14:25.431 --> 00:14:27.967
Again
it will grab the input and output for you.

292
00:14:27.967 --> 00:14:31.003
Nothing really more that you need to do in
this case to trace that particular tool.

293
00:14:31.904 --> 00:14:34.440
And then for your data visualization tool,

294
00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:37.109
again, you can use the decorators as well.

295
00:14:37.109 --> 00:14:39.778
This is another case
where you have a two-step tool.

296
00:14:39.778 --> 00:14:40.479
But in this case

297
00:14:40.479 --> 00:14:43.883
you have multiple steps that are split
across two different methods.

298
00:14:44.283 --> 00:14:46.585
So you can keep things simple
and use the decorators

299
00:14:46.585 --> 00:14:50.222
because those contain
all of the logic for that particular step.

300
00:14:50.823 --> 00:14:53.792
So here for this extract chart config.

301
00:14:53.792 --> 00:14:55.227
This in itself is not a tool.

302
00:14:55.227 --> 00:14:56.629
This is just part of a tool.

303
00:14:56.629 --> 00:14:59.231
So it makes sense to call it chain here.

304
00:14:59.231 --> 00:15:03.869
And so you can say @tracer dot
chain as opposed to @tracer dot tool.

305
00:15:04.003 --> 00:15:05.871
For this extract chart config method.

306
00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:09.642
And then you can do the same kind of thing

307
00:15:09.642 --> 00:15:13.946
for the other method that gets called here
which is your create chart method

308
00:15:14.013 --> 00:15:17.016
in your @tracer dot chain.

309
00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:21.153
And also make sure that you run
any of these cells again

310
00:15:21.654 --> 00:15:24.657
as you added things to them
just to update that.

311
00:15:25.391 --> 00:15:27.760
And then finally
you can go to your last method here

312
00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:31.563
this generate visualization
and mark this as a tool.

313
00:15:32.197 --> 00:15:35.768
So you've marked three different tool
spans for your three different tools.

314
00:15:36.068 --> 00:15:39.071
You've marked a few different chain spans
within those tools.

315
00:15:39.471 --> 00:15:42.541
And you've marked your agent
and router spans as well.

316
00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:45.477
Now you've added
all the tracing that you need to

317
00:15:45.477 --> 00:15:47.513
so you can go back down
and run your agent again,

318
00:15:47.513 --> 00:15:49.782
and you should see some
more detailed information.

319
00:15:51.283 --> 00:15:53.419
And so
now if you jump back over into Phoenix,

320
00:15:53.419 --> 00:15:56.422
you should see you have a new trace
that's been run.

321
00:15:56.755 --> 00:15:58.524
So you should now have a second entry.

322
00:15:58.524 --> 00:16:01.627
And if you click into that, it'll look
slightly different than the one before.

323
00:16:01.627 --> 00:16:03.762
You'll have a few more spans here.

324
00:16:03.762 --> 00:16:07.299
Notably, you'll have this yellow
one of your lookup sales data tool.

325
00:16:07.566 --> 00:16:10.769
So that was one that you captured with
that decorator on your lookup sales data.

326
00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:16.308
And then within that you'll also have
a chain span for executing the SQL query.

327
00:16:16.942 --> 00:16:19.278
And look at the inputs
and outputs of those.

328
00:16:19.278 --> 00:16:21.880
this is the one that's been captured
using the with clause

329
00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:24.717
inside of your lookup sales data method.

330
00:16:24.717 --> 00:16:27.319
And if you ran more complicated queries
that you used other

331
00:16:27.319 --> 00:16:30.556
tools, you would see those tools
appearing as well too.

332
00:16:30.589 --> 00:16:33.826
So now you have all of the information
that you need to understand

333
00:16:33.826 --> 00:16:36.261
what's happening
inside of your agent at any given step.

